//
// GENERATED by cuda/gen_fe32.py -- do not edit the multiply by hand.
//
// ## Why this exists alongside the radix-25.5 implementation
//
// The first implementation used ten signed 25.5-bit limbs accumulated into
// 64-bit registers, following the Ed25519 reference implementation. It is
// easier to get right, because carries are deferred rather than propagated.
// Profiling the search kernel showed the cost of that choice: **56% of the
// kernel's PTX operations were 64-bit** -- 1606 `add.s64`, 1232 `mad.lo.s64`,
// 327 `shr.s64` -- and on this hardware each of those is two 32-bit
// operations. The kernel was spending most of its time synthesising 64-bit
// arithmetic the machine does not have.
//
// This representation uses saturated 32-bit limbs and propagates carries with
// the hardware carry flag, via `mad.lo.cc.u32` / `madc.hi.cc.u32` chains. The
// multiply becomes 64 products in 144 instructions instead of 100 products in
// roughly 590.
//
// ## Representation
//
//     h = h[0] + h[1]*2^32 + h[2]*2^64 + ... + h[7]*2^224
//
// Values are kept below 2^256 and are *not* fully reduced: a value may be
// anywhere in [0, 2^256), and several encodings represent the same field
// element. Canonicalisation happens only in `fe_freeze`, and therefore only in
// `fe_tobytes` and `fe_prefix_be64`. Reduction after each operation folds the
// overflow with the identity 2^256 = 38 (mod 2^255 - 19).
//
// ## The trade against radix 25.5
//
// Addition and subtraction get *more* expensive: they need a real carry chain
// and a fold, where the redundant signed representation could simply add limbs
// and defer. The search kernel does about five multiplications and two
// additions per candidate, so the multiply dominates and the trade pays.

